# ── LINE ENDINGS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every text file is LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than a bare `text=auto` on purpose. `text=auto` alone only
# fixes what lands in the index; the checkout still follows whatever
# core.autocrlf each machine happens to have, so the same clone comes out CRLF
# on one Windows box and LF on the next. That is precisely how this repo ended
# up with an LF index and a CRLF working tree — 130 files reporting as fully
# rewritten in every diff, which makes review impossible and turns any merge
# into a whole-file conflict. Pinning eol here removes the per-machine
# variable instead of asking every contributor to configure git correctly.
#
# No bulk rewrite is needed here: every blob already committed is LF, so this
# file only stops git from re-deriving CRLF on the way out. Working copies
# cloned before it stay CRLF on disk until their next checkout, which is
# cosmetic — git normalises on comparison, so status and diff read clean
# either way. If a tree ever does drift, `git add --renormalize .` restages
# it, and that belongs in its own commit, never mixed with feature work.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── BINARY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only jpg and png are tracked today; the rest are listed pre-emptively
# because the failure is silent and permanent. A font or image with no NUL
# byte in its first 8000 bytes can be misdetected as text, at which point the
# rule above rewrites bytes inside it on checkout and corrupts the asset.
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.pdf   binary
